description:
Line 358-360: The `assigned` bitmap validation happens after canonical form check:
```rust
if assigned.is_some_and(|mask| bitmap & !mask & BITMAP_MASK != 0) {
    return Err(FragmentError::UnassignedBitmapBit);
}
```

However, the canonical form check on lines 353-357 could return `NonCanonicalAck` for an ACK that also has unassigned bits set. This means the error reported depends on serialization details rather than the semantic error.

More critically, line 543-561 in `handle_ack_bytes`:
```rust
let ack = Ack::from_bytes(data)?;  // Does NOT check assigned
if ack.complete {
    return Ok(self.handle_ack(ack));  // Complete ACK bypasses assigned check!
}
...
let ack = Ack::from_bytes_for(data, Some(self.assigned_bitmap(ack.window)))?;
```

A complete ACK is processed without any `assigned` validation, which could allow a malformed ACK with window != final_window to skip validation (though handle_ack does check window).

Suggested fix: For defense in depth, validate assigned mask before canonical check, or document why the current order is intentional.
